module gunbc.spark.serving_boot_provenance


// DURABILITY IS OBSERVED, NOT CAUSED, and that is the whole point of this module.
//
// THE PROBLEM IT REPLACES. "Does the serving unit survive a reboot" was going to be answered by
// rebooting the host: issue `systemctl reboot`, wait, replan, require empty. That needs the managed
// account to hold reboot authority it does not have, and the alternative offered was a human
// rebooting the machine by hand -- out-of-band actuation, which is the failure this repository names
// rather than a cautious choice. Either way the receipt exists only on runs where somebody rebooted.
//
// WHAT REBOOT-TESTING IS ACTUALLY A PROXY FOR. Not "the machine can restart" -- nobody doubts that.
// It is "was this unit started BY SYSTEMD AT BOOT, or by a hand that will not be there next time".
// That is the enablement fact, and it is exactly what an enable-without-start cannot deliver and a
// start-without-enable fakes until the next power cycle.
//
// AND THAT FACT IS DIRECTLY READABLE. systemd records when a unit entered the active state, in
// microseconds on the monotonic clock, which is zeroed at boot. A unit that entered active a few
// seconds into the boot was brought up by the manager during startup; one that entered active hours
// in was started by something else, later. No reboot is required to read it, no privilege is
// required to read it, and both reads are already classified INERT in gunbc.readback_independence
// (`systemctl show` reads the manager's recorded state and starts nothing), so the probe cannot
// establish what it reports.
//
// WHAT THIS BUYS BEYOND THE REBOOT TEST. Any reboot becomes evidence -- an operator's, a power cut,
// a kernel update -- rather than only the ones automation caused. The receipt lands on EVERY
// converge instead of on the rare runs that power-cycled something. And the fleet stops needing a
// standing authority to destroy host availability in order to answer a question about a unit file.
//
// THE HONEST LIMIT, stated because it is real: this establishes that the unit came up WITH THE
// CURRENT BOOT. It cannot manufacture a boot, so a host that has not restarted since the unit was
// enabled reports exactly that -- BootProvenanceUnitPredatesNoBoot -- which is an honest "not yet
// established", never a pass. What it removes is the need to CAUSE the boot, not the need for one to
// happen.

// THE MICROSECONDS ARE CARRIED AS Microsecond, NOT AS Int, and the comment that used to explain the
// scale is why. This module previously said "Both are converted to microseconds so the comparison is
// between like magnitudes rather than between a number and a string that happens to sort" -- a
// sentence doing a type's job. systemd reports ActiveEnterTimestampMonotonic in bare microseconds and
// /proc/uptime reports seconds, so the two arrive at DIFFERENT scales and are made to agree by hand;
// with both sides bare `Int` nothing stops a later edit feeding the seconds value straight in, and the
// comparison would silently be a million times wrong while still typechecking. `Measure<Time, Micro,
// Nat>` makes that unwritable rather than warned against, and `Nat` is the honest carrier besides: a
// monotonic timestamp and an uptime are both non-negative, so the negative half of `Int` was a state
// with no meaning that every consumer had to ignore.
type SparkServingBootProvenance
  = UnitStartedByManagerAtBoot { active_enter_monotonic: std.measure.Microsecond, uptime: std.measure.Microsecond }
  | UnitStartedAfterBoot { active_enter_monotonic: std.measure.Microsecond, uptime: std.measure.Microsecond }
  | UnitNotActiveAtAll
  | BootProvenanceUnobserved { cause: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

fn spark_serving_active_enter_probe_argv() -> List<String> {
  concat(
    ["systemctl", "--user"],
    skip(extdeps.systemd.systemctl.systemctl_show_property_argv(
      unit: gunbc.spark.serving_unit_identity.spark_serving_unit_name as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
      property: extdeps.systemd.ActiveEnterTimestampMonotonic,
    ), n: 1),
  )
}

// THE RELATIONAL ORIGIN. `default.target` is what the user manager reaches when every unit
// wanted by it has come up, and gunbc-spark-serving.service is WantedBy=default.target
// (verified on both hosts, 2026-08-22). So a unit that came up as part of the startup
// transaction necessarily enters active NO LATER than the target does, and one started by hand
// afterwards enters active strictly later. Both sides of that comparison are observed on the
// host; nothing in it is fitted.
fn spark_serving_default_target_enter_probe_argv() -> List<String> {
  concat(
    ["systemctl", "--user"],
    skip(extdeps.systemd.systemctl.systemctl_show_property_argv(
      unit: spark_serving_default_target_name as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
      property: extdeps.systemd.ActiveEnterTimestampMonotonic,
    ), n: 1),
  )
}

data spark_serving_default_target_name: String = "default.target"

fn spark_serving_uptime_probe_argv() -> List<String> {
  ["cat", "/proc/uptime"]
}


// systemd reports the monotonic timestamp as bare microseconds; /proc/uptime reports seconds with
// two decimals. Both are converted to microseconds so the comparison is between like magnitudes
// rather than between a number and a string that happens to sort.
// THE COMPARISON IS RELATIONAL AND HAS NO CONSTANT IN IT, and the constant it replaced was not
// merely mis-fitted -- it compared against the WRONG ORIGIN, which is a different defect and one
// that adjusting the value could never have repaired.
//
// What was here: active_enter <= 10,000,000us, a window measured from KERNEL boot. Measured
// 2026-08-22 on both converged hosts (run 32543249175 and a paired hand probe), the user manager
// does not itself reach active until ~10.6s into kernel boot, because lingering has to bring the
// user session up first. So a user unit that came up PERFECTLY with its manager was GUARANTEED to
// fall outside a kernel-boot window: the check had no true-positive path for the thing it was
// built to detect, and it duly reported both durable hosts as not durable for two days.
//
// Widening the window would have greened both hosts by accident and left the defect intact until a
// host with a 12s session setup hit it. That is the "fitting a constant to a host until the check
// means nothing" the deleted carrier warned about, reached by a route it did not anticipate -- so
// the warning was under-scoped, not wrong.
//
// The relation: the unit came up with the startup transaction exactly when it entered active NO
// LATER than default.target did. Measured 2026-08-22:
//   srv5  unit 10,670,729us  default.target 10,675,297us   inside by 4,568us
//   srv6  unit 10,627,595us  default.target 10,632,705us   inside by 5,110us
// Both sides are observed on the host and neither is authored here. A unit hand-started hours
// later enters active far past the target, so the predicate can still be FALSE -- which is why
// this is a relation and not `entered >= manager_start`, a comparison that would have been
// trivially true for every unit and traded a false negative for silent false positives.
fn spark_serving_classify_boot_provenance(
  active_enter: extdeps.ssh.session.SshSessionExecResult,
  default_target_enter: extdeps.ssh.session.SshSessionExecResult,
  uptime: extdeps.ssh.session.SshSessionExecResult,
) -> SparkServingBootProvenance {
  if active_enter.success == false {
    BootProvenanceUnobserved {
      cause: join([
        "ActiveEnterTimestampMonotonic probe failed exit=", to_string(active_enter.exit_code),
        " stderr=", active_enter.stderr,
      ], "") as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if default_target_enter.success == false {
    BootProvenanceUnobserved {
      cause: join([
        "default.target ActiveEnterTimestampMonotonic probe failed exit=",
        to_string(default_target_enter.exit_code),
        " stderr=", default_target_enter.stderr,
      ], "") as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if uptime.success == false {
    BootProvenanceUnobserved {
      cause: join([
        "/proc/uptime probe failed exit=", to_string(uptime.exit_code),
        " stderr=", uptime.stderr,
      ], "") as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    match parse_int(s: std.algebra.trim(s: active_enter.stdout)) {
      Absent =>
        if std.algebra.trim(s: active_enter.stdout) == "0" || std.algebra.trim(s: active_enter.stdout) == "" {
          UnitNotActiveAtAll
        } else {
          BootProvenanceUnobserved {
            cause: join([
              "ActiveEnterTimestampMonotonic is not a monotonic microsecond count: ",
              std.algebra.trim(s: active_enter.stdout),
            ], "") as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
          }
        }
      Present { value: entered_us } =>
        if entered_us == 0 {
          UnitNotActiveAtAll
        } else {
          match spark_serving_uptime_microseconds(stdout: uptime.stdout) {
            Absent =>
              BootProvenanceUnobserved {
                cause: join(["/proc/uptime is not parseable: ", std.algebra.trim(s: uptime.stdout)], "") as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
              }
            Present { value: up } =>
              match std.checked_arithmetic.checked_int_magnitude(a: entered_us) {
                std.checked_arithmetic.CheckedNatOverflow { cause: _ } =>
                  BootProvenanceUnobserved {
                    cause: "ActiveEnterTimestampMonotonic is not representable as a microsecond count" as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
                  }
                std.checked_arithmetic.CheckedNatReady { value: entered_nat } => {
                  let entered = std.measure.microsecond(count: entered_nat)
                  match parse_int(s: std.algebra.trim(s: default_target_enter.stdout)) {
                    Absent =>
                      BootProvenanceUnobserved {
                        cause: join([
                          "default.target ActiveEnterTimestampMonotonic is not a monotonic microsecond count: ",
                          std.algebra.trim(s: default_target_enter.stdout),
                        ], "") as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
                      }
                    Present { value: target_us } =>
                      if target_us == 0 {
                        BootProvenanceUnobserved {
                          cause: "default.target reports no active-enter, so the startup transaction has no observed end to compare against" as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
                        }
                      } else if entered_nat <= target_us {
                        UnitStartedByManagerAtBoot { active_enter_monotonic: entered, uptime: up }
                      } else {
                        UnitStartedAfterBoot { active_enter_monotonic: entered, uptime: up }
                      }
                  }
                }
              }
          }
        }
    }
  }
}

// /proc/uptime is "SECONDS.FF IDLE\n". Only the whole-second part is taken and scaled: the
// hundredths carry no information at the resolution this comparison works at, and parsing a decimal
// to keep them would be inventing precision the decision does not use.
fn spark_serving_uptime_microseconds(stdout: String) -> std.measure.Microsecond? {
  match split(std.algebra.trim(s: stdout), " ").first() {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: secs_field } =>
      match split(secs_field, ".").first() {
        Absent => none
        Present { value: whole } =>
          match parse_int(s: whole) {
            Absent => none
            Present { value: n } =>
              if n < 0 {
                none
              } else {
                match std.checked_arithmetic.checked_int_magnitude(a: n * 1000000) {
                  std.checked_arithmetic.CheckedNatOverflow { cause: _ } => none
                  std.checked_arithmetic.CheckedNatReady { value: scaled } => Present { value: std.measure.microsecond(count: scaled) }
                }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE ONLY ARM THAT ESTABLISHES DURABILITY, and the three that do not are distinct on purpose. A
// unit started after boot has NOT been shown to survive one; a unit that is not active has not been
// shown to do anything; an unobserved probe has shown nothing at all. Collapsing any of them into
// "not durable" would report a measurement nobody took.
// THIS IS A DIAGNOSTIC, NOT THE DURABILITY RECEIPT, and the distinction was a real error in this
// module's first revision. A window comparison says a unit entered active NEAR boot; a correctly
// enabled user service can legitimately enter active well after any window, because user-manager
// start and lingering are ordered against everything else the boot is doing. A slow boot would
// therefore report a durable host as not durable, and the tempting remedy -- widen the window --
// is fitting a constant to a host until the check means nothing.
//
// gunbc.spark.serving_durability holds the terminal proof: the kernel mints a fresh boot_id every
// boot, so a changed boot_id is the machine stating the previous boot ended rather than an inference
// from timing. What THIS answers, and answers well, is why a host that did reboot came back
// unconverged -- which the boot_id comparison cannot tell you.
fn spark_serving_boot_provenance_establishes_durability(p: SparkServingBootProvenance) -> Bool {
  match p {
    UnitStartedByManagerAtBoot { active_enter_monotonic: _, uptime: _ } => true
    UnitStartedAfterBoot { active_enter_monotonic: _, uptime: _ } => false
    UnitNotActiveAtAll => false
    BootProvenanceUnobserved { cause: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE WIRE STATES WHAT WAS MEASURED, NOT WHAT WAS CONCLUDED. This arm used to print "the unit is
// running but did NOT come up with this boot" -- an assertion of fact the instrument could not
// support and which was, on the live fleet, false: both converged hosts were reported as
// not-durable for two days by a check with no true-positive path (see the classification comment
// above). A diagnostic stating a conclusion its own carrier cannot reach is fabricated plausible
// output at the wire layer, DESIGN section 5, and this one propagated into operator reports.
//
// Now that the comparison is relational, the wire can say the measured thing plainly: whether the
// unit entered active before or after default.target. That is a fact about one observed ordering,
// not an inference about a boot nobody watched. Note what it still does NOT claim -- that the
// host would survive a reboot. Coming up with the manager on THIS boot is evidence for auto-start,
// and full reboot durability still needs a boot_id compared across an actual boot.
fn spark_serving_boot_provenance_wire(p: SparkServingBootProvenance) -> String {
  match p {
    UnitStartedByManagerAtBoot { active_enter_monotonic: e, uptime: u } =>
      join([
        "boot-provenance=started-by-manager-at-boot active_enter_us=", to_string(std.measure.microsecond_count(e)),
        " uptime_us=", to_string(std.measure.microsecond_count(u)),
        " -- the unit entered active no later than default.target, so it came up WITH the manager startup transaction",
      ], "")
    UnitStartedAfterBoot { active_enter_monotonic: e, uptime: u } =>
      join([
        "boot-provenance=started-after-boot active_enter_us=", to_string(std.measure.microsecond_count(e)),
        " uptime_us=", to_string(std.measure.microsecond_count(u)),
        " -- the unit entered active AFTER default.target did, so it was not part of the manager startup transaction",
      ], "")
    UnitNotActiveAtAll => "boot-provenance=unit-not-active"
    BootProvenanceUnobserved { cause: c } =>
      join(["boot-provenance=unobserved ", c as String], "")
  }
}
